<Docs
  title="Mini-apps and extensions"
  description="Small, purpose-built panes that manage their own slice of your lists — a travel checklist or a pipeline, instead of a tag convention you have to remember."
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="what">What they are</h2>
  <p>
    A mini-app is a panel in the app that owns a specific kind of work. It appears as its own area rather
    than hiding inside the general task list, which is the difference between "I have a tag for trips" and
    "I have a trips pane that knows what a trip needs".
  </p>
  <p>
    They are extensions in the useful sense: they bring their own vocabulary and their own defaults, but
    they write into the same local database as everything else, so a task created by a mini-app is still a
    task you can move, tag, and schedule.
  </p>

  <h2 id="travel">Travel checklists</h2>
  <p>
    Travel is the one that ships today. <Badge tone="success" variant="subtle">available</Badge> Give it a
    trip and it produces the checklist that kind of trip needs — the packing list, the pre-departure
    chores, the things that only matter for that destination — as regular tasks in the right place.
  </p>
  <p>
    The value is in the template, not the storage: the list of what a trip needs is the part people forget,
    and the app is where you already look at lists.
  </p>
  <p>
    Creating one is a short form: a name (leave it blank and the app derives one like "Hiking, Swimming ·
    5-day"), a trip length, and the activities you plan. The checklist then appears in sections — packing
    groups plus "before leaving" chores — with clothing scaled to the trip length and extras for longer
    stays, such as laundry reminders. Finishing every item completes the trip.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some tasks in a mini-app pane carry a lock: those are managed by the app itself and only their
    completion can be changed, while the rest remain fully editable. Either way, everything is still an
    ordinary task in your lists.
  </p>

  <h2 id="planned">Planned</h2>
  <ul>
    {
      planned.map((item) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{item.name}</strong> — {item.note} <Badge tone="warning" variant="subtle">coming soon</Badge>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model">How they are modelled</h2>
  <p>
    Mini-apps are built on the same primitives the rest of the app uses — managed tags, sections, and tasks
    — rather than on a private store of their own. That is a deliberate constraint: it means a mini-app can
    never hold your data hostage, and its tasks show up in every view that already works.
  </p>
  <p>
    The model is specified in <code>docs/spec/todo2-managed-apps-spec.md</code>; the
    <a href="/docs/contributor/architecture#specs">spec table</a> has its status, and
    <a href="/docs/status">Feature status</a> tracks what is shipped.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="tip" title="Working on one">
    Mini-apps are the most approachable part of the app to extend: a pane, a template, and the storage
    layer's managed entities. Start at <a href="/docs/contributor">the contributor guide</a>.
  </Callout>
</Docs>
